  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Hazardous driving conditions due to reduced \nvisibility in dense fog across the west-central Alleghenies \nearly this morning\n\nCombination of IR satellite and ASOS+RWIS observations prompted\nthe issuance of a dense fog advisory (DFA) for a portion of the\nwest-central Alleghenies early this morning. With astronomical \nsummer officially underway (as of 424AM EDT) and the shortest \nnight of the year nearly over, expect dense fog to dissipate by\n8AM. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Locally heavy rainfall and strong to marginally \nsevere thunderstorm wind gusts remain possible over south\ncentral PA Monday afternoon/evening  \n\nExcessive rainfall concerns remain focused over south central PA\non Monday PM where best overlap of high pwats and instability \nwill exist per HREF/REFS. This area also remains in a level 2 of\n4 ERO from WPC. A limiting factor is the ongoing dryness and \nD1/D2 drought conditions over the LSV. This setup most likely \nfavors urban areas with the best chance to experience flash \nflooding. Rain rates of 1-2 in/hr could lead to localized 2+ \ninch amounts over a short duration - and in the right location \nwould result in at least some runoff issues. QPF has trended \nlower on the margin, so there is still some uncertainty in play \nwith decreasing instability with northern extent. In terms of \nsevere storm potential, the best combination of shear and CAPE \nis located just south of the MD line where SPC has placed a \nlevel 2/SLGT risk. The exact location of the warm sector will be\na key factor and ultimately modulate overall severe storm risk.",
